      "Westchester kitchens from the 60s and 70s were built for a different era. Small, closed off, with cabinets that barely hold a week's groceries for a family. If you cook in your Westchester kitchen every day, and most families here do, you know the frustration. Not enough counter space. Bad lighting. Cabinets that are falling apart. We have remodeled so many Westchester kitchens that we can spot the model just by walking in the door. The single-story homes along Coral Way and Bird Road share similar layouts. We know which walls can come down, where the plumbing sits, and how to make the most of the available space. The Cuban coffee window stays if you want it. The rest gets upgraded. New cabinets with actual storage. Quartz countertops that give you room to roll out dough and prep a full meal at the same time. Lighting that lets you see what you are doing. These Westchester homes are where families gather, and the kitchen is the center of it. We build kitchens that honor that tradition while bringing everything else into the present.",
